<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Añaquito</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1546">1546</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Iñaquito</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Añaquito was fought on 18 January 1546 on the plain of Iñaquito near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> between the forces of Viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blasco-nunez-vela">Blasco Núñez Vela</a> and the rebel encomenderos led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-pizarro">Gonzalo Pizarro</a>. The rebels won, and the viceroy, the first appointed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, was killed and beheaded on the field. The victory left Pizarro master of Peru until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-la-gasca">Pedro de la Gasca</a> defeated him at Jaquijahuana in 1548.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-anaquito.jpg" alt="Battle of Añaquito" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Trabajo propio. Reproducido de un dibujo de autor desconocido. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batalla_de_A%C3%B1aquito.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The viceroyalty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and the Royal Audience of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> were created in 1542, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blasco-nunez-vela">Blasco Núñez Vela</a> arrived to enforce the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-laws">New Laws</a>, which abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomienda</a> and banned the personal labour of the indigenous people. The encomenderos chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-pizarro">Gonzalo Pizarro</a>, then a wealthy encomendero in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Charcas</a>, to lead their protest. In Lima the viceroy made himself hated, killing the official Illán Suárez de Carbajal with his own hands, and on 18 September 1544 the judges of the Audience deposed him and shipped him away. Pizarro entered Lima on 28 October 1544 with 1,200 soldiers and was received as governor.</p><p>The viceroy persuaded his keeper, the judge Juan Álvarez, to free him, landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumbes">Tumbes</a> and raised an army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>. After a long pursuit as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popayan">Popayán</a>, Pizarro drew him out by pretending to march south against the royalist rising of Diego Centeno in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Charcas</a>, while in fact waiting near Quito.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Pizarro had about 700 men, 200 of them with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a> and 150 mounted, against the viceroy's 400 or so with some 140 horse. The viceroy wore an uncu, an indigenous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> shirt, over his armour. His musketeers opened the battle and he led a cavalry charge, but Pizarro's musketeers and larger infantry decided the day. Sebastián de Benalcázar was wounded and the viceroy's infantry broke once its captains fell. The viceroy was struck in the head by Hernando de Torres; Benito Suárez de Carbajal, brother of the murdered Illán, had him beheaded and his head raised on a pike.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>About 300 of the viceroy's men died against seven of the rebels. Benalcázar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-hernandez-giron">Francisco Hernández Girón</a> were pardoned, and a few of the viceroy's most stubborn followers were hanged or exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>. The head was dragged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> and set on the pillory, but leading residents had the body and head buried in the cathedral of Quito; they were later taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avila">Ávila</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Iñaquito” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The first viceroy, Blasco Núñez Vela, arrived in 1544, but his attempt to enforce the New Laws provoked a revolt of encomenderos under Gonzalo Pizarro, who defeated and killed him at the <b>Battle of Añaquito</b> in 1546.</blockquote><a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
